1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,122
Liquid is a template language of Orchard Core.

2
00:00:04,122 --> 00:00:09,525
Themes, content and settings use it to print values, change them and make decisions.

3
00:00:09,525 --> 00:00:14,028
In this video, you learn the syntax, the objects, the filters and the

4
00:00:14,028 --> 00:00:16,550
tags, and the options of the parser.

5
00:00:21,150 --> 00:00:26,779
Themes and modules keep Liquid templates in files with the .liquid extension, in a Views folder.

6
00:00:26,779 --> 00:00:30,974
The Blog theme has a layout template for the whole page, and templates

7
00:00:30,974 --> 00:00:34,230
for some of its content types, such as the blog post.

8
00:00:34,980 --> 00:00:39,285
The Templates module lets you write the same kind of template in the admin.

9
00:00:39,285 --> 00:00:43,218
The Liquid part and the Liquid field keep Liquid in content, and the

10
00:00:43,218 --> 00:00:48,160
Autoroute part builds URLs from a Liquid pattern, as the Blog recipe does for articles.

11
00:00:48,910 --> 00:00:53,309
Editing a Liquid part or a Liquid field needs the Manage Liquid templates

12
00:00:53,309 --> 00:00:56,372
permission, which is marked as security-critical.

13
00:00:56,372 --> 00:01:00,047
Writing a template in the admin also needs Manage templates.

14
00:01:00,047 --> 00:01:03,500
The Administrator and Editor roles have both by default.

15
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:11,870
Two curly braces print a value, such as the title of a content item.

16
00:01:11,870 --> 00:01:15,928
A pipe sends the value through a filter, which can take arguments.

17
00:01:15,928 --> 00:01:19,817
A tag opens with a curly brace and a percent sign, and controls

18
00:01:19,817 --> 00:01:21,620
what the template renders.

19
00:01:22,370 --> 00:01:24,541
A dot reads a property of an object.

20
00:01:24,541 --> 00:01:28,263
In this blog post template, the title is a property of the content

21
00:01:28,263 --> 00:01:32,450
item, and the subtitle is read through the part and the field that hold it.

22
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:36,969
A template encodes what it prints as HTML by default.

23
00:01:36,969 --> 00:01:40,227
To write markup as it is, add the raw filter.

24
00:01:40,227 --> 00:01:44,890
To clean the markup first, run the sanitize_html filter before raw.

25
00:01:49,089 --> 00:01:51,256
Let's open a blog post on the site.

26
00:01:51,256 --> 00:01:55,327
The Blog theme draws this page with its Liquid templates, so the values

27
00:01:55,327 --> 00:01:57,125
on it come from expressions.

28
00:01:57,125 --> 00:02:02,359
The chapters after this one explain the objects, filters and tags that those expressions use.

29
00:02:02,810 --> 00:02:06,705
In the navigation bar, the name of the site is the SiteName property

30
00:02:06,705 --> 00:02:08,021
of the Site object.

31
00:02:08,021 --> 00:02:11,810
The link around it goes to the home page, through the href filter.

32
00:02:12,275 --> 00:02:15,605
The large title is the DisplayText of the content item.

33
00:02:15,605 --> 00:02:19,045
The line under it is the Subtitle field of the blog post.

34
00:02:19,495 --> 00:02:23,176
Under the subtitle, Posted by is a text that the t filter looks

35
00:02:23,176 --> 00:02:25,950
up in the translations of the current culture.

36
00:02:25,950 --> 00:02:29,045
The name after it is the Author of the content item.

37
00:02:33,245 --> 00:02:38,484
The Model object is the shape being displayed, the view model of the template.

38
00:02:38,484 --> 00:02:43,848
On a content page, its ContentItem property is the content item, with properties

39
00:02:43,848 --> 00:02:47,715
such as DisplayText, ContentType, CreatedUtc and Author.

40
00:02:48,465 --> 00:02:53,440
The Content property of a content item holds its parts and fields as a document.

41
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:57,142
The fields that you add to a content type sit under a part

42
00:02:57,142 --> 00:03:01,885
named after the type, so the subtitle is BlogPost, then Subtitle, then Text.

43
00:03:02,635 --> 00:03:11,015
The Liquid Core Services feature, which is always enabled, provides Culture, Request, Environment, HttpContext and TrackingConsent.

44
00:03:11,015 --> 00:03:15,908
Culture has the name and direction of the current culture, and Request has

45
00:03:15,908 --> 00:03:18,905
the path, the query string and the headers.

46
00:03:19,655 --> 00:03:21,820
Other features add objects.

47
00:03:21,820 --> 00:03:27,855
Settings adds Site, with SiteName and PageSize, and Contents adds Content, which loads

48
00:03:27,855 --> 00:03:30,741
content items by identifier or handle.

49
00:03:30,741 --> 00:03:35,989
Users adds User, whose Identity holds the name of the signed-in user, when

50
00:03:35,989 --> 00:03:38,875
the Liquid feature is enabled as well.

51
00:03:43,075 --> 00:03:47,821
A filter changes the value on its left, and filters chain from left to right.

52
00:03:47,821 --> 00:03:51,996
This pattern from the Blog recipe takes the title of a content item

53
00:03:51,996 --> 00:03:54,455
and turns it into a segment of a URL.

54
00:03:55,205 --> 00:04:02,255
Standard Liquid filters, such as date, upcase and default, are available everywhere.

55
00:04:02,255 --> 00:04:08,052
The Liquid module adds filters of its own, such as t, slugify, href,

56
00:04:08,052 --> 00:04:11,185
absolute_url, local, utc and json.

57
00:04:11,935 --> 00:04:16,816
The t filter translates a text with the current culture, and its arguments

58
00:04:16,816 --> 00:04:19,073
fill the numbered placeholders.

59
00:04:19,073 --> 00:04:23,282
The href filter turns a virtual path into a URL for the tenant,

60
00:04:23,282 --> 00:04:26,455
and absolute_url adds the scheme and the host.

61
00:04:27,205 --> 00:04:29,923
Dates in content items are stored in UTC.

62
00:04:29,923 --> 00:04:33,335
The local filter converts one to the local time zone:

63
00:04:33,335 --> 00:04:36,978
the time zone of the site, or the one of a signed-in user

64
00:04:36,978 --> 00:04:39,117
when User Time Zone is enabled.

65
00:04:39,117 --> 00:04:41,315
The utc filter converts it back.

66
00:04:42,065 --> 00:04:45,677
Other features add the filters for what they own.

67
00:04:45,677 --> 00:04:48,895
Contents adds display_text and display_url.

68
00:04:48,895 --> 00:04:54,805
Users adds has_permission, is_in_role and user_email, which work on the User object.

69
00:04:55,555 --> 00:04:59,243
A filter exists only while its feature is enabled.

70
00:04:59,243 --> 00:05:04,249
The shortcode filter needs Shortcodes, and markdownify needs Markdown.

71
00:05:04,249 --> 00:05:08,135
A separate video covers writing a filter of your own.

72
00:05:12,335 --> 00:05:14,772
Standard Liquid tags handle the logic.

73
00:05:14,772 --> 00:05:19,093
The blog post template assigns the first image path to a variable, tests

74
00:05:19,093 --> 00:05:22,416
whether it is nil, and chooses which header to render.

75
00:05:22,416 --> 00:05:25,075
The for tag repeats a block for each item.

76
00:05:25,825 --> 00:05:28,721
Liquid Core Services adds tags of its own.

77
00:05:28,721 --> 00:05:33,728
The form tag calls the ASP.NET Core form tag helper, and the antiforgerytoken

78
00:05:33,728 --> 00:05:36,865
tag writes the hidden field that a form needs.

79
00:05:36,865 --> 00:05:40,605
The helper and block tags call a tag helper by its name.

80
00:05:41,355 --> 00:05:46,828
The cache tag keeps the output of its block when Dynamic Cache is enabled.

81
00:05:46,828 --> 00:05:51,754
The Resources module adds script, style, link, meta and resources.

82
00:05:51,754 --> 00:05:56,475
A separate video covers the tags for layouts, shapes and zones.

83
00:06:00,675 --> 00:06:05,181
The parser belongs to Liquid Core Services, a feature that is always enabled.

84
00:06:05,181 --> 00:06:09,143
Three options of the parser are read from the Liquid section of the

85
00:06:09,143 --> 00:06:12,075
configuration, and all three are off by default.

86
00:06:12,825 --> 00:06:17,911
In the appsettings file, under OrchardCore and Liquid, set an option to true.

87
00:06:17,911 --> 00:06:25,570
AllowParentheses allows parentheses in templates, AllowLiquidTag turns on the liquid tag, and AllowTrailingQuestionMark

88
00:06:25,570 --> 00:06:28,205
lets a name end with a question mark.

89
00:06:30,205 --> 00:06:35,330
Liquid reads values from objects, changes them with filters and controls the output

90
00:06:35,330 --> 00:06:38,498
with tags, and each enabled feature adds its own.

91
00:06:38,498 --> 00:06:39,995
Thanks for watching.

